# Layers.City iOS app

This repo is the iOS app (SwiftUI app + WidgetKit extension) for Layers.City, the maps at
https://layers.city/. The website lives in a separate repo that this project must never
change, clone or fetch from.

## What the app is (read first)
- `APP_BRIEF.md` in this repo says what the app should and shouldn't be. The app was restarted
  from it on October 20, 2026; don't carry over code or screens from the first attempt unless the
  brief calls for them.
- The app is the glance, the website is the deep tool: widgets, a few notifications, big numbers
  and short phrases. Never aim for parity with the web map; link to layers.city instead.
- Design each feature for the lock screen widget first.
- Every feature works for every city from its `city.json`, and hides itself where a city has no
  data for it. No city names in code.

## Where the data comes from
- Read `DATA_CONTRACT.md` in this repo before writing any code that loads or shows map data.
  It is the source of truth for file names, fields and how the "What's here" card is built.
- Load data only over HTTPS from `https://layers.city/<city>/data/<file>`.
  Never from github.com, raw.githubusercontent.com or a local copy of the website repo.
- Start from each city's `city.json`. Read layer ids, files, card rows and labels from it;
  don't hard-code them. Check `"schema"`: if it isn't a version the app knows, keep the last
  good cached copy and show an "update the app" message.
- Ignore unknown keys and files. New ones are not errors.
- Ship a bundled copy of each city's data for offline use and refresh it in the background.
- Colours come from `palette` in `city.json` (light and dark per CSS variable name). Don't copy
  hex values into the app.
- Maps are secondary. Where the brief calls for a small map, section 11 of `DATA_CONTRACT.md`
  says how the website styles layers and how to adapt that to Apple's map. Polygons have holes;
  use `MKGeoJSONDecoder` and keep `interiorPolygons`.

## Privacy (not negotiable)
- Location, saved places and the neighbourhood timeline stay on the device, synced only through
  CloudKit's private database (the user's own iCloud). No accounts or sign-in (including Sign in
  with Apple), no server that receives location or identity, no ad or analytics SDKs.
- With iCloud unavailable, keep working on-device and offer Export / Import of a backup file.
- Ask for each permission (Always location, notifications) only when the user turns on the
  feature that needs it, with a one-line reason. Every feature must still work without it.
- Schedule notifications locally from published data wherever possible.
- One clear control to wipe the timeline and saved places.

## Display rules (from the website; keep them)
- Describe, don't rank. Language at home and any other demographic field is shown on the card
  only. Never use it for colours, sorting, filters, rankings, comparisons or notifications.
- Always show the caveat and footer lines from `city.json` next to census figures, including
  in widgets (a shortened form is fine).
- No crime data, school scores or other evaluative data.
- Show the `attribution` from `city.json` in the app (an About screen is fine).
- Real-time data (weather, air quality, traffic, beach water) comes from its own sources, is
  labelled with its source, and is kept visually separate from the census and boundary card.

## Working style
- Small, focused changes; explain what changed and why in each commit message.
- If the contract seems wrong or missing something, stop and say so instead of guessing or
  working around it. Changes to the data are made on the website side, not here.
